[86.847477, "o", "If you do not understand the fundamentals, it is virtually \r\nimpossible. However, once you understand and imple- \r\nment the basics, the results will follow. The goal of all \r\nSMT process engineers is to repeatedly produce high \r\nquality solder joints that have fine grain structure and \r\nexcellent wetting characteristics, as shown in the figure \r\nbelow: \r\nSURFACES MUST BE \r\nCLEAN AND SOLDERABLE \r\nII MUST BE \r\n11 SMALL \r\n--\r\nHigh quality, low defect soldering \r\nrequires identifying the optimum \r\ntemperature profile for reflowing \r\nthe solder paste, and repeating it \r\nover and over. \r\nI WETTING ; ‘ TIME , \r\n"]
[86.847538, "o", "30-60 .’ : SEC ~ \r\nTYPICAL PROFILE \r\nA typical profile consists of a preheat, dryout, and reflow \r\nsection (zone) where different heating rates are used. In \r\nthe preheat section, the goal is to fully preheat the SMT \r\n--\r\nelevated to a temperature greater than the melting point \r\nof the solder. For 63/37 Eutectic solder, the melting \r\ntemperature is 183°C. This temperature must be ex- \r\nceeded by approximately 20°C to ensure quality reflow. \r\nThe amount of time the solder joint is above the melting \r\npoint is referred to as the “wetting time”. The wetting \r\ntime is normally 30 to 60 seconds for most pastes. If the \r\nwetting time is excessive, intermetallics may occur in \r\n"]
[86.847705, "o", "the joint, which result in brittle solder joints. In sum- \r\nmary, define the profile, and hit it. \r\nSection I - Technology 3 \r\nHOW MANY HEATING ZONES ARE REQUIRED? \r\nThis question frequently arrives and can be a source of \r\n--\r\nrates. In the reflow zone, insufficient heat will result in \r\nnon-reflowed or partially reflowed solder joints. The \r\njoints will have a dull, rough surface finish in this case. \r\nExcessive heating in the reflow zone may result in burnt \r\nboards, damaged components, or possibly solder \r\ndewetting. In a dewet solder joint, the solder is reflowed \r\nbut it is pulled away from the pad or the component lead. \r\nDEFINE THE BAND \r\nThe profile band needs to be defined for the given SMT \r\nassembly. To define this band, one should be aware of \r\nnot only the solder paste requirements, but also any "]
[86.847759, "o", "\r\n--\r\nReflow of low-residue \r\nsolder paste \r\n< 100 ppm \r\nThe paper presents a number of excellent graphs that \r\nshow the affect of nitrogen purity on parameters includ- \r\ning flux spread, wetting force, wetting time, solder balls, \r\ncleaning, residue, board discoloration, and micro-struc- \r\nture. It is recommended reading if your process may \r\nrequire the use of nitrogen. You must determine the \r\nnitrogen requirements for your process. The benefits of \r\nusing nitrogen definitely exist, however you must deter- \r\n"]
[86.847898, "o", "\u001b[?2004hroot@3fc7c37f9150:/app# "]
[149.356417, "i", "cat <<EOF > solve_task.py\nimport pandas as pd\nimport numpy as np\nimport json\n\n# Load Data\nmes = pd.read_csv('/app/data/mes_log.csv')\ntcs = pd.read_csv('/app/data/thermocouples.csv')\ndefects = pd.read_csv('/app/data/test_defects.csv')\n\n# Helper to save JSON\ndef save_json(data, filename):\n    with open(f'/app/output/{filename}', 'w') as f:\n        json.dump(data, f, indent=2)\n\n# --- Q1: Preheat Ramp Rate ---\n# Definition: Preheat 100-150C. Limit < 2.0 C/s. Critical TC: smallest_mass (fastest heating).\n\nramp_limit = 2.0\nviolating_runs_q1 = []\nmax_ramp_by_run = {}\n\nfor run_id in mes['run_id'].unique():\n    run_tcs = tcs[tcs['run_id'] == run_id]\n    if run_tcs.empty:\n        continue\n    \n    # Select representative TC: smallest_mass for thermal shock\n    tc_data = run_tcs[run_tcs['tc_location'] == 'smallest_mass']\n    if tc_data.empty:\n        # Fallback if smallest_mass not present (though expected)\n        tc_data = run_tcs[run_tcs['tc_location'] == 'largest_mass']\n        if tc_data.empty:\n             tc_data = run_tcs # Use whatever is there\n             \n    # Iterate over unique TCs in this selection (should be 1 usually)\n    # If multiple TCs have smallest_mass, pick the one with highest max ramp?\n    # Or just process the first one? Let's process each and take the worst case (max ramp).\n    \n    max_ramp_run = -1.0\n    selected_tc_id = None\n    \n    for tc_id in tc_data['tc_id'].unique():\n        df_tc = tc_data[tc_data['tc_id'] == tc_id].sort_values('time_s')\n        \n        # Filter for Preheat region 100 <= T <= 150\n        # We need to find the segments where T is in range.\n        # Calculate derivative between points.\n        \n        times = df_tc['time_s'].values\n        temps = df_tc['temp_c'].values\n        \n        # Calculate instantaneous ramp rates\n        # We only consider ramp rates where the interval is within the preheat zone?\n        # Or the temperature is within the zone?\n        # \"during preheat\".\n        \n        ramps = []\n        for i in range(len(times) - 1):\n            t1, t2 = times[i], times[i+1]\n            temp1, temp2 = temps[i], temps[i+1]\n            \n            # Check if this segment is in preheat\n            # We assume preheat is the phase where temp is rising from ambient to soak/reflow.\n            # The handbook says \"preheat section... temperatures generally between 100C to 150C\".\n            # So we check if the segment overlaps with 100-150 range?\n            # Or strictly if both points are in range? \n            # If one point is 90 and next is 110, the ramp applies.\n            # Let's consider any segment where the average temp is in 100-150 or either point is in range.\n            # To be safe and strict: Check if the segment contributes to the rise in the 100-150 range.\n            \n            if (temp1 >= 100 and temp1 <= 150) or (temp2 >= 100 and temp2 <= 150):\n                 if t2 > t1:\n                     rate = (temp2 - temp1) / (t2 - t1)\n                     ramps.append(rate)\n        \n        if ramps:\n            max_r = max(ramps)\n            if max_r > max_ramp_run:\n                max_ramp_run = max_r\n                selected_tc_id = tc_id\n    \n    if selected_tc_id:\n        max_ramp_by_run[run_id] = {\n            \"tc_id\": selected_tc_id,\n            \"max_preheat_ramp_c_per_s\": round(max_ramp_run, 2)\n        }\n        if max_ramp_run > ramp_limit:\n            violating_runs_q1.append(run_id)\n\n# Sort\nviolating_runs_q1.sort()\n# Sort dict by key\nmax_ramp_by_run = dict(sorted(max_ramp_by_run.items()))\n\nq1_output = {\n  \"ramp_rate_limit_c_per_s\": ramp_limit,\n  \"violating_runs\": violating_runs_q1,\n  \"max_ramp_by_run\": max_ramp_by_run\n}\nsave_json(q1_output, 'q01.json')\n\n\n# --- Q2: TAL ---\n# TAL Window: 30-60s. TC: largest_mass (coldest, hardest to reflow).\n# Threshold: Liquidus.\n\nq2_output = []\n\nfor index, row in mes.iterrows():\n    run_id = row['run_id']\n    liquidus = row['solder_liquidus_c']\n    \n    run_tcs = tcs[tcs['run_id'] == run_id]\n    if run_tcs.empty:\n        continue\n        \n    # Select largest_mass\n    tc_data = run_tcs[run_tcs['tc_locat"]
[149.356631, "i", "ion'] == 'largest_mass']\n    if tc_data.empty:\n        tc_data = run_tcs # Fallback\n    \n    # If multiple largest_mass, which one? The one with MINIMUM TAL (worst case for wetting)?\n    # Or the one with MAXIMUM TAL (worst case for brittle)?\n    # Handbook says \"ensure quality reflow... wetting time is normally 30 to 60\".\n    # If TAL < 30 -> non-compliant (didn't wet).\n    # If TAL > 60 -> non-compliant (brittle).\n    # So we need to check if ANY largest_mass TC is out of spec? Or the representative one?\n    # \"Select the most representative thermocouple value\".\n    # Usually, largest mass is the critical one for MIN TAL.\n    # Let's pick the specific TC that has the lowest Peak Temp (coldest) as the representative for largest_mass?\n    # Or just process all largest_mass TCs and report the one that fails? \n    # The output format expects one entry per run (implied by \"tc_id\": \"\").\n    # Let's calculate TAL for all largest_mass TCs and pick the one that is most critical.\n    # Most critical = furthest from center of window? Or min TAL?\n    # Let's pick the one with Min TAL as it's the \"coldest\" spot.\n    \n    selected_tc = None\n    min_tal = float('inf')\n    \n    # First, calculate TAL for all candidates\n    candidates = []\n    for tc_id in tc_data['tc_id'].unique():\n        df_tc = tc_data[tc_data['tc_id'] == tc_id].sort_values('time_s')\n        times = df_tc['time_s'].values\n        temps = df_tc['temp_c'].values\n        \n        # Calculate time above liquidus\n        # Simple integration: sum of time intervals where avg temp > liquidus\n        tal = 0.0\n        for i in range(len(times) - 1):\n            t1, t2 = times[i], times[i+1]\n            temp1, temp2 = temps[i], temps[i+1]\n            \n            # Check intersection\n            if temp1 > liquidus and temp2 > liquidus:\n                tal += (t2 - t1)\n            elif temp1 <= liquidus and temp2 <= liquidus:\n                continue\n            else:\n                # Linear interpolation\n                # T(t) = m*t + c\n                # Find t where T = liquidus\n                slope = (temp2 - temp1) / (t2 - t1)\n                if slope != 0:\n                    t_cross = t1 + (liquidus - temp1) / slope\n                    if temp1 > liquidus:\n                         tal += (t_cross - t1)\n                    else:\n                         tal += (t2 - t_cross)\n        \n        candidates.append({'tc_id': tc_id, 'tal': tal})\n    \n    # Select representative.\n    # If any is < 30, pick that (failure).\n    # If all > 60, pick that (failure).\n    # If multiple fails, pick worst.\n    # If all pass, pick any (maybe min TAL).\n    \n    best_candidate = candidates[0]\n    # Logic: Prioritize showing non-compliance.\n    # Check for < 30\n    low_fails = [c for c in candidates if c['tal'] < 30]\n    high_fails = [c for c in candidates if c['tal'] > 60]\n    \n    if low_fails:\n        best_candidate = min(low_fails, key=lambda x: x['tal']) # Worst low fail\n    elif high_fails:\n        best_candidate = max(high_fails, key=lambda x: x['tal']) # Worst high fail\n    else:\n        # All compliant, pick min TAL (closest to limit?)\n        best_candidate = min(candidates, key=lambda x: x['tal'])\n        \n    status = \"compliant\"\n    if best_candidate['tal'] < 30 or best_candidate['tal'] > 60:\n        status = \"non-compliant\"\n        \n    q2_output.append({\n        \"run_id\": run_id,\n        \"tc_id\": best_candidate['tc_id'],\n        \"tal_s\": round(best_candidate['tal'], 2),\n        \"required_min_tal_s\": 30.0,\n        \"required_max_tal_s\": 60.0,\n        \"status\": status\n    })\n\nq2_output.sort(key=lambda x: x['run_id'])\nsave_json(q2_output, 'q02.json')\n\n\n# --- Q3: Peak Temp ---\n# Min Peak = Liquidus + 20. TC: largest_mass.\n\nfailing_runs_q3 = []\nmin_peak_by_run = {}\n\nfor index, row in mes.iterrows():\n    run_id = row['run_id']\n    liquidus = row['solder_liquidus_c']\n    required_peak = liquidus + 20.0\n    \n    run_tcs = tcs[tcs['run_id'] == run_id]\n    if run_tcs.empty:\n        failing_runs_q3.append(run_id)\n        continue\n        \n    tc_data = run_tcs[run_tcs['tc_location'] "]
[149.356721, "i", "== 'largest_mass']\n    if tc_data.empty:\n        tc_data = run_tcs\n        \n    # Find the TC with the LOWEST peak temp (worst case for min peak req)\n    worst_peak = float('inf')\n    selected_tc = None\n    \n    for tc_id in tc_data['tc_id'].unique():\n        peak = tc_data[tc_data['tc_id'] == tc_id]['temp_c'].max()\n        if peak < worst_peak:\n            worst_peak = peak\n            selected_tc = tc_id\n            \n    min_peak_by_run[run_id] = {\n        \"tc_id\": selected_tc,\n        \"peak_temp_c\": round(worst_peak, 2),\n        \"required_min_peak_c\": round(required_peak, 2)\n    }\n    \n    if worst_peak < required_peak:\n        failing_runs_q3.append(run_id)\n\nfailing_runs_q3.sort()\nmin_peak_by_run = dict(sorted(min_peak_by_run.items()))\n\nq3_output = {\n  \"failing_runs\": failing_runs_q3,\n  \"min_peak_by_run\": min_peak_by_run\n}\nsave_json(q3_output, 'q03.json')\n\n\n# --- Q4: Conveyor Speed ---\n# Req Speed = Boards/Min * Length / LoadFactor\n\nq4_output = []\n\nfor index, row in mes.iterrows():\n    run_id = row['run_id']\n    bpm = row['boards_per_min']\n    length = row['board_length_cm']\n    lf = row['loading_factor']\n    actual_speed = row['conveyor_speed_cm_min']\n    \n    if lf > 0:\n        req_speed = (bpm * length) / lf\n    else:\n        req_speed = 0 # Should not happen\n        \n    meets = actual_speed >= (req_speed - 0.01) # Tolerance?\n    \n    q4_output.append({\n        \"run_id\": run_id,\n        \"required_min_speed_cm_min\": round(req_speed, 2),\n        \"actual_speed_cm_min\": round(actual_speed, 2),\n        \"meets\": meets\n    })\n\nq4_output.sort(key=lambda x: x['run_id'])\nsave_json(q4_output, 'q04.json')\n\n\n# --- Q5: Best Run ---\n# Best run per board_family.\n# Criteria: Pass all checks (Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4). Then Efficiency (Speed?)\n# Also Quality (Defects).\n\n# 1. Identify failing runs\nfailed_runs = set()\nfailed_runs.update(violating_runs_q1)\nfailed_runs.update([r['run_id'] for r in q2_output if r['status'] == 'non-compliant'])\nfailed_runs.update(failing_runs_q3)\nfailed_runs.update([r['run_id'] for r in q4_output if not r['meets']])\n\n# 2. Defect counts\n# Sum defects per run\ndefect_counts = defects.groupby('run_id')['count'].sum().to_dict()\n\n# 3. Group by family\nfamilies = mes['board_family'].unique()\nq5_output = []\n\nfor family in families:\n    family_runs = mes[mes['board_family'] == family]\n    \n    # Candidates\n    candidates = []\n    for idx, row in family_runs.iterrows():\n        rid = row['run_id']\n        is_compliant = rid not in failed_runs\n        d_count = defect_counts.get(rid, 0)\n        speed = row['conveyor_speed_cm_min']\n        \n        candidates.append({\n            'run_id': rid,\n            'compliant': is_compliant,\n            'defects': d_count,\n            'speed': speed\n        })\n    \n    # Sort candidates\n    # Priority: 1. Compliant (True > False)\n    #           2. Defects (Low > High)\n    #           3. Speed (High > Low)\n    \n    candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (x['compliant'], -x['defects'], x['speed']), reverse=True)\n    \n    if candidates:\n        best_run = candidates[0]['run_id']\n        runner_ups = [c['run_id'] for c in candidates[1:]]\n        runner_ups.sort() # List others\n        \n        q5_output.append({\n            \"board_family\": family,\n            \"best_run_id\": best_run,\n            \"runner_up_run_ids\": runner_ups\n        })\n\nq5_output.sort(key=lambda x: x['board_family'])\nsave_json(q5_output, 'q05.json')\n\nEOF\n"]
